A||||6||JohnnyK||Wheel refinishing||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||08-06-2000||03:17 AM||||I have read lots of the articles on aluminum wheel refinishing, and they all say to use a paint thinner to get the clear coat off. Well, my car has been sitting for quite some time and the wheels look horrible. There is none of that grey stuff (there is a bit, but it is peeling) and they are pitted and stuff. My question is, why do I need to use paint stripper? I tried sanding and it looks like with a bit of work it will turn out nicely. Is this ok, or am I missing something? Paint stripping seems like a lot of work as I would have to take the tire off the rim, and I am not prepared to do this. Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Brian||08-06-2000||07:47 AM||||Hi Johhny,<P>While I've never sanded the clearcoat I'll be you're gonna go through a fair bit of paper do to the "clog" factor. The paint "stripper"<BR>is much easier. Slather the stuff on, let sit an hour or so & wipe off. Repeat if nessesary. As for removing the tire from the rim?..you could have the tire broken down, pull the stem & weights & replace them later.<BR>Or just work very carefully around the stem, the weights though have got to go & that means a balancing job any how.<P>Regards,<BR>Brian<P>&gt;My question is, why do I need to use paint &gt;stripper? I tried sanding and it looks like &gt;with a bit of work it will turn out nicely. &gt;Is this ok, or am I missing something? Paint &gt;stripping seems like a lot of work as I &gt;would have to take the tire off the rim, and &gt;I am not prepared to do this. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||08-06-2000||08:08 AM||||I have refinished my GT wheels and they came out perfect. If you do a search on wheel refinishing you'll see my thread. I used "Permatex Spray-On Gasket Remover" to remove clear coat and paint. I had no tires on my wheels but a friend stripped and refinished his with the tires on using the same stuff I used. The thread also lists everything needed to recoat the wheels.<P>Here's the thread:<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002367.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002367.html</A> <P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 08-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||rprevo||08-06-2000||10:23 AM||||What I did with mine is used a D.A.<BR>(Orbital sander)And 80 grit, Used about<BR>10 disks total on all four wheels then<BR>polished the aluminum with a 400 grit<BR>on the D.A. then I painted the wheel<BR>all black so I could fill in the cracks<BR>crevices that I wanted black and sanded<BR>the paint off in the unwanted area's of the<BR>rim. Then I used mothers and a buffing<BR>wheel to really shine the Aluminum. Then<BR>I shot it with clear. The reason for the<BR>400 grit sandpaper is it really puts a shine<BR>on aluminum or magniesium witch ever they are.<BR> <BR>Stripper wont take out the pits but 80 grit<BR>takes it right down. The job was about 8<BR>hrs or so but actual cost was maybe 20 bucks<BR>and they look brand new.<BR> <BR> Ray<P> ||||reg||6
Z||000004||rogergarrison||08-06-2000||10:37 AM||||when i redo customer wheels, and on my own car, i use stripper, and you dont have to removed tires, it wont hurn rubber. When its all peeled off, is sand off any nicks, etc with 80 grit then again with 400 or 600 wet or dry. I dont clearcoat after. they can be polished or like my own car, i clean them up with scotchbrite. I like the machined, not polished look, myself. And now never have to worry about clear peeling again. If you clear them and ever put on new tires or get them rebalanced the clear will start peeling where the old weights were. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||08-06-2000||11:02 AM||||I just finished two of the wheels on my 87 GT. The center part looked OK so I just did the rim. I used paint stripper and just brushed it on carefully. They look excellent. I'm not going to clearcoat them, just keep them polished.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnnyK||08-06-2000||02:37 PM||||Well, the valve is leaking and the weights are rusty (you can tell shes a beaut  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> So I removed the weights, and the valve doesn't matter, so I'm gonna try sanding today. What about a wire brush (to get rid of the crevices) and a palm sander? Also, can I polish, and then clear coat?||||reg||1
